// 1. .NET vs C# in three sentences:
// .NET is a platform: a runtime called the CLR, a large standard library, and the
// tooling to build and package code. C# is a programming language whose compiler
// emits IL, a portable bytecode that the CLR executes. Other languages such as F#
// target the same platform, which is why .NET is not "a C# framework".

// 2. Why one binary runs on multiple operating systems:
// The compiled file contains IL rather than native machine code, and each platform
// ships its own CLR that JIT-compiles that IL to local machine code at runtime.

